# program entrypoint contract
# ---
# provides the platform/arch-specific `_start` symbol that the linker uses as
# the program entrypoint. user code must define a `main` function as follows:
#
#   use std.runtime;
#
#   #[symbol("main")]
#   fun main(argc: i64, argv: **u8) i64 {
#       ret 0;
#   }
#
# the `_start` entrypoint gathers argc/argv from the host and calls the user's
# `main`. argv holds argc arguments and a closing nil. on windows it is the wide
# command line split into UTF-8 (std.runtime.windows.cmdline). before main it also
# keeps the arguments in the host's native units (std.runtime.native). on
# return, it terminates the process with the exit code returned by `main`.
#
# on a freestanding target there is no host. `_start` sets the stack pointer to
# `__run_stop_.stack`, copies `.data` to where it runs and zeroes `.bss`, all
# before any mach code, then calls `main(0, nil)` and parks the core when it
# returns (std.runtime.freestanding). a freestanding wasm module's host lays
# nothing out but what the module states, so its `_start` calls `main(0, nil)`
# and returns, trapping when main returns nonzero
# (std.runtime.freestanding.wasm32).
#
# on wasi the host calls `_start` with nothing, so it asks the host for the
# arguments and the environment, and ends the process through `proc_exit`
# (std.runtime.wasi).
#
# on uefi the firmware calls `efi_main` with the image handle and the system
# table. it keeps both for the program, calls `main(0, nil)`, and returns main's
# result to the firmware as the image's status (std.runtime.uefi).
#
# on linux, darwin and windows the runtime sets threads up before main through
# `mach_thread_setup`, a link-time default (`#[weak]`). on linux it reads the
# executable's thread-local template, records whether std owns the thread
# pointer, and installs the main thread's block where std owns it. on darwin
# and windows it installs the main thread's block. a program replaces it with a
# strong definition of the name, and an empty one drops the setup, and all that
# only it reaches, from the link:
#
#   #[symbol("mach_thread_setup")]
#   fun thread_setup() {}
#
# std's own allocator, printing and exit work without the setup: std reads a
# thread-local only on a thread std started, and only once std has started one
# (std.sync.thread.any_started), so a program that never spawns reads none.
# nothing checks what such a program uses beyond that. it gives up:
# - on linux, its own thread-locals: a `#[thread]` var is reached through a
#   thread pointer nothing set, which faults on x86_64 and reads stray memory
#   on aarch64, riscv64 and riscv32. std.sync.thread.current_token reads the
#   same thread pointer and is given up with them. std.sync.thread.spawn takes
#   the loader-hosted path, so it returns unsupported unless a thread backend is
#   in the link (std.system.os.linux.hosted). once a backend has started a
#   thread, std reads thread-locals on the main thread too (a shared heap's
#   bindings), so in an image no interpreter started, where nothing set the
#   main thread's thread pointer, the main thread does not allocate from a
#   shared heap after the first spawn. the main thread's stack bounds hold, and the
#   exit wipe reads no thread pointer when no template was read.
# - on darwin and windows, only the main thread's block: thread-locals and
#   spawning work as before, the main thread's token comes from its thread id,
#   and its stack bounds from libpthread or the TEB, as on a thread std did not
#   create.
#
# note: `use std.runtime;` is required to link the entrypoint into the binary.

$if ($mach.build.sys == $mach.sys.linux) {
    use std.runtime.linux;
}
$or ($mach.build.sys == $mach.sys.darwin) {
    use std.runtime.darwin;
}
$or ($mach.build.sys == $mach.sys.windows) {
    use std.runtime.windows;
}
$or ($mach.build.sys == $mach.sys.freestanding) {
    use std.runtime.freestanding;
}
$or ($mach.build.sys == $mach.sys.wasi) {
    use std.runtime.wasi;
}
$or ($mach.build.sys == $mach.sys.uefi) {
    use std.runtime.uefi;
}
$or {
    $error("std.runtime needs a linux, darwin, windows, wasi, uefi or freestanding target");
}
